{ Black against the queen pawn. You build one house, with pawns on d5 and e6, and it stands up to everything he tries. }
1. d4 { He opens with the queen pawn. Meet it head on: your own queen pawn goes two squares, to d5. [%cal Yd7d5] } 1... d5 { Your pawn stands in the middle and stops his from going any further. Against a quiet pawn to e3, the house is the same: knight out, pawn to e6, castle. [%csl Gd5] } (1... Nf6 { Just as good, and a road that often comes back to our house by another door. } 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 d5 { Level: here it reached exactly the position of our main line. He gets more ways to steer away, so we start with the d pawn. [%csl Gd5] }) (1... e6 { Just as good, but against his king pawn to e4 it turns into the French, a whole different opening. } 2. e4 d5 { Now it is the French, a different opening, with his pawns on e4 and d4 side by side. } 3. Nc3 Nf6 { About level, and a whole different fight. Today we build the house with the d pawn first. [%csl Be6] }) (1... c6 { A fine move, and it leads to the Slav, where the c pawn props up the d pawn. } 2. c4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 { About level: a good opening of its own, just not ours. [%csl Bc6] }) (1... d6 { Almost as good, but it lets his king pawn step up beside the queen pawn, and he takes the middle. } 2. e4 { His king pawn steps up beside the queen pawn, and he owns the centre. } 2... Nf6 3. Nc3 { About level, but his two pawns rule the middle and yours wait behind them. [%csl Rd4,Re4] }) (1... c5 { A sharp punch, but his pawn just walks past to d5 and grabs space. } 2. d5 Nf6 3. Nc3 { About level: this is the Benoni, a fighting opening of its own, with his pawn on d5 cramping you. [%csl Rd5] }) (1... e5 { A gambit: your king pawn steps up, and his queen pawn simply takes it. } 2. dxe5 Nc6 3. Nf3 { Better for him: you are a pawn down, and his knight guards the extra pawn on e5. [%csl Re5] }) (1... f5 { Playable: that is the Dutch, where the f pawn grabs e4 instead of fighting for d5. } 2. g3 Nf6 3. Bg2 { About level: a sharp opening of its own, with your king a little more open. [%csl Bf5] }) 2. c4 { The Queen's Gambit: he offers his c pawn to pull yours away from the middle. Say no thanks, and prop your pawn up with the king pawn. [%cal Ye7e6] } 2... e6 { That is the Queen's Gambit Declined: you turn the pawn down, and the pawn on e6 keeps yours standing on d5. [%csl Ge6,Gd5] } (2... c6 { Just as good: that is the Slav, where the c pawn props up d5 instead. Another opening. } 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 cxd5 { Level: his d pawn and yours stand face to face, and the c column is open. Our line props d5 up with the king pawn. [%csl Gd5] }) (2... dxc4 { The gambit accepted, and quite playable, but you will not keep that pawn. } 3. e4 { He grabs the middle with both pawns while your pawn sits on c4. } 3... e5 4. Nf3 { About level: you are a pawn up for now, but he wins it back whenever he likes. We keep our pawn in the middle instead. [%csl Rc4] }) (2... c5 { About as good, and sharp: you hit his centre at once, though your queen has to come out to take back. } 3. cxd5 Qxd5 4. Nf3 cxd4 5. Nc3 { His knight comes out and hits your queen, so she has to move again. } 5... Qa5 6. Nxd4 { About level, but his pieces came out while your queen walked about. [%csl Ra5] }) (2... Nc6 { About as good, though while this knight sits in front of your c pawn, that pawn cannot prop up the middle. } 3. Nf3 e6 4. Nc3 Nf6 { About level, and the pawn on c7 is stuck behind your own knight. [%csl Rc7] }) (2... Nf6 { About as good: a natural move, though no pawn props up d5, so he simply takes it. } 3. cxd5 Nxd5 4. e4 { His king pawn kicks your knight away and takes the middle. } 4... Nf6 5. Nc3 { About level, but his two pawns own the centre, and your knight was pushed back to f6. [%csl Rd4,Re4] }) (2... Bf5 { The light bishop comes out first, but he swaps on d5, and your bishop ends up trading itself for his knight. } 3. cxd5 Bxb1 4. Rxb1 Qxd5 5. Nf3 { Slightly better for him: your queen came out early, and he has the two bishops. [%csl Rd5] }) (2... Bg4 { The bishop comes out, but there is no knight to pin yet, and your d pawn has no prop. } 3. cxd5 Qxd5 4. h3 Bh5 5. Nc3 Qa5 { Better for him: his knight came out with a hit on your queen, and your bishop stands far away on h5. [%csl Rh5] }) 3. Nc3 { He brings a knight out to lean on d5. Whichever knight comes first, a knight move gets the same reply: your king knight to f6. [%cal Yg8f6] } 3... Nf6 { Your knight guards d5 and gets ready for castling. Three of your men now guard that pawn, and only two of his hit it. [%csl Gd5] } (3... c5 { Just as good: that is the Tarrasch, where your c pawn hits back at once. } 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Nf3 Nc6 { About level, but your d pawn can end up standing alone. A different opening. [%csl Bd5] }) (3... Be7 { Just as good: the bishop first, and the knight follows. } 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bf4 Nf6 6. e3 { About level: when his bishop came out, your knight was not yet on f6 to pin, so the bishop took the road to f4. [%csl Bf4] }) (3... c6 { Every bit as good, and a whole opening of its own, the Semi-Slav. } 4. Nf3 Nf6 { About level: a solid wall, but your light bishop stays shut in for a long time. [%csl Bc8] }) (3... Bb4 { Just as good: your bishop pins the knight that leans on d5. It is a sharper road. } 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bf4 { About level: a fine position, with his bishop out on f4 and the game much sharper. [%csl Bb4] }) (3... dxc4 { About as good, though you give up the middle: now he takes it with his king pawn. } 4. e4 c5 5. d5 { About level, but his pawns on d5 and e4 cramp you, and he wins the c pawn back later. [%csl Rd5] }) (3... Bd6 { The bishop goes to d6, but there it blocks your queen's guard of d5. [%cal Rd8d5] } 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Nxd5 { His knight takes the pawn on d5, because your queen no longer guards it. } 5... Nf6 6. Nc3 { Slightly better for him: you are a pawn down for nothing. [%csl Rd5] }) (3... a6 { Just as good: a quiet pawn move that keeps his pieces off b5. The fight for d5 waits a move. } 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Nf3 Nf6 { About level: playable, but that pawn move did not help your pieces come out. [%csl Ba6] }) (3... h6 { Just as good: a little pawn move first, so his bishop can never come to g5. } 4. Bf4 Nf6 { About level: his bishop simply found another good square on f4. [%csl Bf4] }) 4. Bg5 { The pin: his bishop hits your knight, and your queen stands right behind it. Step your dark bishop into the gap. Against a quiet pawn to e3, the same bishop move. [%cal Rg5d8,Yf8e7] } 4... Be7 { The bishop blocks the pin. Your knight is free to move again, and your king is one step from castling. [%csl Ge7] } (4... Nbd7 { About as good, and a little cunning: the second knight guards the pinned one. } 5. cxd5 exd5 6. e3 c6 7. Bd3 { About level, a fine set-up. We block the pin with the bishop, the simplest way. [%csl Bd7] }) (4... Bb4 { Just as good: a pin against a pin. Your bishop leans on the knight that leans on d5. } 5. e3 c5 { About level, and very sharp. Ours is the calm road. [%csl Bb4] }) (4... c6 { Just as good: the c pawn props up d5, though it does nothing about the pin. } 5. e3 Nbd7 6. cxd5 exd5 7. Bd3 { About level: you end up guarding the knight with another piece anyway. [%csl Bc6] }) (4... dxc4 { Just as good, though once his e pawn steps up, his light bishop wins the c pawn back and comes out at the same time. } 5. e3 c5 6. Bxf6 Qxf6 7. Bxc4 { Level, and a lot more to remember than our calm road. [%csl Bc4] }) (4... c5 { Just as good, and very sharp: you hit his centre while his bishop pins your knight. } 5. cxd5 cxd4 6. Qxd4 Be7 7. e4 { About level: his pawn on d5 is extra for now, and he can keep it, but your pieces come out fast as payment. [%csl Rd5] }) (4... h6 { Too early. His bishop takes your knight, and your d pawn loses a guard. } 5. Bxf6 Qxf6 6. cxd5 { He swaps on d5, and your e pawn has to take back. } 6... exd5 7. Nxd5 { His knight wins the pawn on d5, because your knight is gone. } 7... Qd8 { Better for him: you are a pawn down. Ask the bishop with the h pawn only once d5 is safe. [%csl Rd5] }) (4... Bd6 { The bishop goes to d6, but it leaves the pin alone and blocks your queen from guarding d5. [%cal Rd8d5] } 5. cxd5 exd5 6. Bxf6 Qxf6 7. Nxd5 Qd8 { Better for him: his knight won your pawn on d5. [%csl Rd5] }) 5. e3 { He builds a small wall with the pawn on e3. Your king has waited long enough: castle. Against his knight to f3 first, castle too. [%cal Ye8g8] } 5... O-O { Your king is tucked away in the corner, behind three pawns, and your rook can join the game. [%csl Gg8] } (5... h6 { Just as good: you ask the bishop first and castle next. } 6. Bh4 O-O 7. Nf3 { Level: the very same position as our main line, reached in another order. [%csl Gh6] }) (5... Nbd7 { Just as good: a useful knight move, and your king can castle next. } 6. Nf3 O-O 7. Rc1 { Level: a solid set-up, though this knight blocks your light bishop for a while. [%csl Bd7] }) (5... c6 { Just as good: the c pawn props up d5 once more, though your king still waits. } 6. Nf3 Nbd7 7. Qc2 { Level: a sound wall, though your king is still in the middle. [%csl Be8] }) (5... dxc4 { Just as good, though you take the c pawn before his light bishop has moved, so it takes back in one move. } 6. Bxc4 c5 7. Nf3 { Level: his bishop came out with a capture instead of losing time, and your c pawn hits his centre at once. [%csl Bc4] }) (5... b6 { About as good: a plan for your light bishop, though your king is still in the middle. } 6. cxd5 Nxd5 7. Bxe7 Qxe7 8. Nxd5 exd5 { About level: the trades came, but your king never castled. [%csl Be8] }) (5... e5 { Too early: this push opens the middle while your pieces are still at home. } 6. dxe5 Ne4 7. Bxe7 Qxe7 8. Nxd5 { His knight jumps into d5, takes a pawn and hits your queen as well. } 8... Qxe5 { Better for him: you are a pawn down, and his knight on d5 rules the middle. [%csl Rd5] }) (5... a6 { Just as good: a small pawn move that keeps his pieces off b5, though your king still waits. } 6. cxd5 exd5 { About level: the pawns swapped in the middle, and your king has not castled yet. [%csl Be8] }) 6. Nf3 { His knight comes out. Now ask his bishop a question with your h pawn. Against a bishop move to d3 instead, take on c4 at once: his bishop has to move twice. [%cal Yh7h6] } 6... h6 { The pawn asks: take my knight, or step back? Either way, your king gets a little window to breathe. [%csl Rg5] } (6... b6 { Just as good: your light bishop gets ready to come out on b7. } 7. Rc1 Bb7 8. cxd5 exd5 { About level: a sound plan, with more theory to learn than our trades. [%csl Bb6] }) (6... Nbd7 { Just as good, the oldest way: the knight comes out before anything else. } 7. Qc2 c6 8. Bd3 { Level: very solid, though your light bishop stays at home. [%csl Bc8] }) (6... Ne4 { Just as good: the trade at once, without asking the bishop first. } 7. Bxe7 Qxe7 8. cxd5 Nxc3 9. bxc3 exd5 { Level: the same kind of trades, only your king has no window. [%csl Bh7] }) (6... dxc4 { About as good, though you take on c4 before his light bishop has moved, so it takes back in one move. } 7. Bxc4 c5 8. O-O { Level: his bishop came out for free, and both kings are safe. [%csl Bc4] }) (6... c6 { About as good: the c pawn gives d5 one more guard, though your light bishop stays shut in. } 7. Bd3 dxc4 8. Bxc4 Nbd7 9. O-O { About level: a solid wall with little room for your pieces. [%csl Bc8] }) (6... c5 { About as good: a punch at his centre, and the queens may soon come off. } 7. dxc5 dxc4 8. Bxc4 Qxd1+ 9. Rxd1 Bxc5 { About level: the queens are off, and his pieces are a little quicker. [%csl Bd1] }) 7. Bh4 { He keeps his bishop. Now the knight jumps forward: it hits his knight on c3 and opens the road for your bishop to meet his. [%cal Yf6e4] } 7... Ne4 { The knight jumps in. Your bishop on e7 now looks straight at his bishop, so he must trade or lose time. Trades give your pieces room. [%cal Ge7h4] } (7... b6 { Just as good: your light bishop heads for b7. This plan has its own name and lots of theory. } 8. Rc1 Bb7 9. cxd5 Nxd5 10. Bxe7 Qxe7 11. Nxd5 exd5 { Level: a fine game, with a few more ideas to learn than our trades. [%csl Gb7] }) (7... Nbd7 { Just as good: a calm knight move before anything else. } 8. Rc1 c5 9. cxd5 Nxd5 10. Bxe7 Nxe7 { Level: a pair of bishops came off anyway, just in a different way. [%csl Ge7] }) (7... c5 { Just as good: a punch at his centre, and the queens come off. } 8. dxc5 dxc4 9. Bxc4 Qxd1+ 10. Rxd1 Bxc5 { Level, with the queens gone, and his pieces are a touch quicker. [%csl Bd1] }) (7... c6 { Just as good: the c pawn props up d5 again. } 8. Be2 dxc4 9. Bxc4 { Level: his bishop took back on c4, and your light bishop still sits at home. [%csl Bc8] }) (7... dxc4 { Just as good, though you take on c4 while his light bishop is still at home, so it takes back in one move. } 8. Bxc4 c5 9. O-O { Level, but his bishop came out for free with that capture. [%csl Bc4] }) 8. Bxe7 { He trades bishops. Take back with the queen. [%cal Yd8e7] } 8... Qxe7 { One pair of pieces is gone. If he swaps on d5 now, trade on c3 first, then take back with the e pawn. [%csl Ge7] } (8... Nxc3 { A trick: you take his knight first. But his bishop is attacking your queen. } 9. Bxd8 { He takes your queen at once. } 9... Nxd1 10. Be7 { His bishop escapes, and now it attacks your rook on f8. } 10... Nxb2 11. Bxf8 Kxf8 { Better for him: he has a rook against your knight, and the pawn you grabbed does not make up the difference. [%csl Rb2] }) 9. Rc1 { His rook comes to the c column. Against a bishop move or a queen move here, you would trade on c3 at once. This time, prop your d pawn up with the c pawn. [%cal Yc7c6] } 9... c6 { Now the d pawn has another guard, and if the c column ever opens, his rook meets your pawn on c6 instead of an open road. [%csl Gd5,Gc6] } (9... Nxc3 { Just as good: the trade at once, and the c pawn comes next. } 10. Rxc3 c6 11. Qc2 Nd7 12. cxd5 exd5 13. Bd3 { Level: the same kind of game, with the pawns swapped in the middle. [%csl Gd5] }) (9... Nf6 { About as good: the knight steps back to f6, and the trades are off. } 10. cxd5 exd5 11. Bd3 c6 12. O-O { About level: you spent two moves on a knight that ended up back on f6. [%csl Rf6] }) (9... b6 { About as good: your light bishop wants b7, though once the c column opens, his rook will stare at your pawn on c7. [%csl Rc7] } 10. cxd5 Nxc3 11. Rxc3 exd5 12. Be2 Bb7 { About level, but his rook now looks at your pawn on c7. [%csl Rc7] }) (9... Nd7 { A natural knight move, but the knight on d7 cuts your queen off from the pawn on c7. [%cal Re7c7] } 10. cxd5 Nxc3 11. Rxc3 exd5 12. Rxc7 { Better for him: his rook ate the pawn on c7. [%csl Rc7] }) (9... dxc4 { Look again: your knight on e4 loses its guard. } 10. Nxe4 { Lost for you: his knight takes yours for free. [%cal Rc3e4] }) (9... c5 { A punch at his centre, but it comes too soon: everything swaps off and your d pawn is left alone. } 10. cxd5 Nxc3 11. Rxc3 cxd4 12. Qxd4 exd5 { Slightly better for him: your pawn on d5 has no neighbours, and his queen already hits it. [%csl Rd5] }) (9... Rd8 { Just as good: the rook stands behind your d pawn and backs it up. } 10. Bd3 Nf6 { About level: the trades are off for now, and your knight went back to f6. [%csl Bf6] }) (9... a5 { Just as good: the a pawn takes two steps, and the trade on c3 can still come. } 10. Bd3 Nxc3 11. Rxc3 { About level: the same trade as the main line, with your a pawn two steps up. [%csl Ba5] }) 10. Bd3 { His bishop hits your knight. Trade it off on c3 first. Had he taken on e4 instead, your d pawn would take back, and the f pawn would hold it. [%cal Ye4c3] } 10... Nxc3 { Your knight on e4 was hit twice and guarded once, so it takes his knight on c3 first, and he has to take back. [%csl Gc3] } (10... f5 { A wall around your knight, but it leaves a big hole on e5 and blocks your light bishop for good. } 11. O-O Nd7 12. Qc2 { Slightly better for him: your light bishop is buried behind the pawns on e6 and f5. [%csl Re6,Rf5] }) (10... Nf6 { Also fine: the knight steps back to f6 instead of trading. } 11. O-O Nbd7 12. Qc2 dxc4 13. Bxc4 { About level, but the trades that free your game never came. [%csl Rf6] }) (10... dxc4 { Look again: your knight on e4 is attacked by his bishop. } 11. Bxe4 { Lost for you: his bishop takes your knight, and a pawn does not pay for it. [%cal Rd3e4] }) (10... Nd7 { Look again: your knight on e4 is attacked, and this move leaves it there. } 11. Nxe4 dxe4 12. Bxe4 { Better for him: the knights came off, and his bishop picked up your pawn on e4. [%csl Re4] }) (10... e5 { The e pawn steps up before your knight has come out to guard it. } 11. Nxe5 { His knight grabs the pawn on e5. } 11... Nd7 12. Nxd7 Nxc3 13. Rxc3 Bxd7 { Better for him: the trades are done, and you are a pawn down. [%csl Re5] }) 11. Rxc3 { His rook takes back. His light bishop has moved now, so take the c pawn: he must spend another move to win it back. Had his b pawn taken back, the same plan. [%cal Yd5c4] } 11... dxc4 { You took on c4 only after his bishop had moved, so the bishop has to move again to take back. That is a free move for you. [%csl Gc4] } (11... Nd7 { Just as good: the knight first, and the swap in the middle can come later. } 12. cxd5 exd5 13. O-O Nf6 { Level: the pawns swapped on d5, and your light bishop has an open road. [%csl Gc8] }) (11... Rd8 { Just as good: the rook stands behind your d pawn, though there is no hurry for it. } 12. Qc2 dxc4 13. Bxc4 Nd7 14. O-O { About level: the same kind of game, with your rook a little early. [%csl Bd8] }) (11... e5 { Too early: his knight and his d pawn both hit e5, and only your queen guards it. } 12. Nxe5 { His knight eats the pawn on e5. } 12... dxc4 13. Bxc4 { Better for him: you are a pawn down. [%csl Re5] }) 12. Bxc4 { His bishop takes back. Bring your last knight out to d7, where it guards the e5 square. [%cal Yb8d7,Gd7e5] } 12... Nd7 { The knight is out, and it guards e5, so your e pawn can step up later without being taken. [%csl Ge5] } (12... b6 { About as good: your light bishop wants b7, though his knight can jump to e5 first and hit your c pawn. } 13. Ne5 { His knight lands on e5 and hits your pawn on c6. } 13... Bb7 14. O-O Nd7 15. Nxd7 Qxd7 { About level, but his knight jumped in before yours came out. [%csl Re5] }) (12... b5 { It kicks his bishop, but it leaves your c pawn weak. } 13. Be2 a6 14. O-O { Better for him: no pawn of yours can ever guard c6 now, and his rook stares at it down the c column. [%csl Rc6] }) (12... e5 { The right idea, one move early: his knight and d pawn hit e5, and your queen is its only guard. [%cal Rf3e5,Rd4e5] } 13. Nxe5 { Better for him: his knight takes the pawn on e5 for free. First the knight to d7, then this push. [%cal Rf3e5] }) (12... Rd8 { Just as good: the rook takes the d column and stares at his d pawn, and your knight comes out next. } 13. Qc2 Nd7 14. O-O e5 { About level: you got there in the end, one move behind. [%csl Bd8] }) (12... c5 { A punch at his centre, but he simply takes the pawn. } 13. dxc5 Rd8 14. Qc2 { Slightly better for him: he keeps his extra pawn, because if your queen takes on c5, his bishop takes on e6 and his rook hits her. [%cal Rc4e6,Rc3c5] }) (12... a5 { Just as good: a quiet pawn step on the queen side first. } 13. Qc2 Nd7 { Level: your knight still comes out to d7, one move later. [%csl Gd7] }) 13. O-O { His king is safe too. Now free your light bishop, the piece stuck behind your pawns: the b pawn steps up, so it can come out to b7. [%cal Yb7b6,Gc8b7] } 13... b6 { The road to b7 is open. Your light bishop was the problem piece of this opening, and now it has a way out. [%cal Gc8b7] } (13... e5 { Just as good, and very popular: the other way to free the light bishop. } 14. Bb3 exd4 15. exd4 Nf6 { About level: your light bishop's road toward f5 and g4 is open now. [%cal Gc8g4] }) (13... c5 { Just as good: a punch at his centre, and the bishop comes out next. } 14. Bb5 cxd4 15. Qxd4 { Level: the centre is open, and your light bishop can come out. [%csl Gc8] }) (13... Nb6 { The knight hits his bishop, but on b6 it blocks your b pawn, so your light bishop has no road to b7. } 14. Bd3 Nd5 15. Rc1 { About level: your knight is centralized, but your light bishop is still stuck. [%csl Rc8] }) (13... Nf6 { The knight hops over to f6, and your light bishop still has no road. } 14. Qe2 { About level: a solid position, but you have not solved your problem bishop. [%csl Rc8] }) (13... a5 { Just as good: the a pawn steps up before anything else. } 14. Be2 e5 { Level: your e pawn has stepped up, and once the knight on d7 moves, your light bishop has a road. [%cal Gc8g4] }) (13... Rd8 { Just as good: the rook takes the d column, ready to face his d pawn once your knight on d7 moves. } 14. Bd3 e5 { About level: your e pawn hits his centre, with the rook right behind the fight. [%csl Ge5] }) (13... b5 { Just as good: the b pawn kicks his bishop at once. } 14. Be2 Bb7 { About level: your bishop is out on b7, and his rook stares at your c pawn. [%cal Rc3c6] }) 14. Bd3 { His rook eyes your c pawn, unguarded since the b pawn moved. Push it to c5: the b pawn guards it there, and it hits his centre. Against a queen move, the bishop goes to b7. [%cal Rc3c6,Yc6c5] } 14... c5 { Your c pawn hits his centre, and your light bishop can still come out to b7. Two pairs of minor pieces are gone, and you are level: that is the Queen's Gambit Declined. [%csl Gc5] } (14... Bb7 { Just as good: your light bishop comes out to b7 and guards the c pawn. } 15. Be4 Rac8 { Level: your rook gives the c pawn a second guard, and your light bishop is out. [%csl Gc6] }) (14... Nf6 { Look again: your c pawn has no guard. } 15. Rxc6 { His rook eats the pawn on c6. } 15... Bb7 16. Rc1 { Better for him: your c pawn is gone, and his rook stepped back to c1 safe and sound. [%csl Rc6] }) (14... e5 { The e pawn steps up, but his bishop can jump to f5 and swap off your knight on d7, the piece that guards the e pawn. } 15. Bf5 exd4 16. Nxd4 { Slightly better for him: your pawn on c6 is hit by his rook and his knight, and nothing of yours guards it. [%csl Rc6] }) 0-1


[Event "Black against d4: he takes on e4"]
[Result "0-1"]
