{ Your knight stands on e4 in the middle of his camp, and the dark bishops are already traded. }
9. Nxe4 { He takes your knight at once. Keeping the tension with his rook to c1 was a little stronger. Take it back. } 9... dxe4 { Your d pawn takes back, and now it hits his knight on f3. If that knight jumps forward to e5 instead of going back, hit his centre with your c pawn. } (9... Qb4+ { A check first, but it costs you a piece: he blocks with his queen, the queens come off, and his knight on e4 escapes. } 10. Qd2 Qxd2+ 11. Nexd2 { Lost for you: the queens came off, his knight escaped, and you never took it back. You are a whole knight down. [%csl Rd2] }) 10. Nd2 { His knight steps back to d2 and hits your pawn on e4, which has no guard. How do you deal with it? } 10... e5 { Your e pawn hits his d pawn, and now he has to look after his own centre. The pawn on e4 looks free to take, but it is bait. [%cal Ge5d4] } (10... f5 { Just as good, and the most popular choice: the f pawn guards e4 at once. [%cal Gf5e4] } 11. Be2 e5 12. d5 { About level, and a touch easier for you: the same wall of pawns, built in the other order. [%csl Ge4,Gf5] }) (10... c5 { A punch from the side, but it leaves the pawn on e4 to his knight. } 11. Nxe4 { His knight takes the pawn on e4. } 11... Rd8 12. Nxc5 e5 { Level, even two pawns down: your rook and your e pawn hit his centre while his king is still in the middle. [%csl Re1] }) (10... c6 { A quiet pawn move that does nothing for the pawn on e4. } 11. Nxe4 e5 12. d5 cxd5 13. Qxd5 { About level, but a pawn down: his queen sits in the middle of the board. [%csl Rd5] }) (10... Nd7 { A natural developing move, but it leaves e4 without a guard. } 11. Nxe4 { His knight takes the pawn. } 11... e5 12. d5 f5 13. Nc3 { Slightly better for him: your pawns got going one move too late, and he is a pawn up. [%csl Re4] }) (10... Qb4 { Your queen hits his knight and the b pawn, but both are easy to guard. } 11. Qc2 e5 12. a3 { His a pawn chases your queen away. } 12... Qb6 { Slightly better for him: your queen went for a walk, and the pawn on e4 is still loose. [%csl Re4] }) (10... b6 { Your light bishop wants b7, but the pawn on e4 drops first. } 11. Nxe4 Rd8 12. Nc3 Bb7 { Slightly better for him: your bishop got its road, but you are a pawn down. [%csl Re4] }) (10... Nc6 { The knight comes out, but it forgets your loose pawn on e4. } 11. Nxe4 { His knight takes the pawn on e4 for free. } 11... b6 12. Be2 { Better for him: he is a pawn up, and his knight sits in the middle. [%csl Re4] }) (10... Rd8 { The rook comes to the d column, but it leaves your pawn on e4 without a guard. } 11. Nxe4 c5 { Level: he took the pawn, and your rook and c pawn hit back at his centre at once. [%csl Gc5] }) 11. d5 { His d pawn steps past yours. Had he taken on e5, your queen would simply take back. Now your pawn on e4 is attacked again: what now? } 11... f5 { The f pawn guards e4. Your three pawns in the middle stand like a wall, and his knight on d2 finds no way back into the centre. You have the easier game. [%csl Ge5,Ge4,Gf5] } (11... Bf5 { Just as good: the bishop guards e4 instead of the f pawn. [%cal Gf5e4] } 12. Be2 Nd7 13. O-O { Level: the bishop holds e4, and your other pieces come out behind it. [%csl Gf5] }) (11... c6 { You hit his d pawn, but this lets his knight take on e4. } 12. Nxe4 cxd5 13. cxd5 Qb4+ { A check, and your queen also eyes his loose pawn on b2. } 14. Nd2 Qxb2 15. Bc4 { About level: the pawns are even again, but his pawn on d5 has no enemy pawn in front of it, and his bishop is out. [%csl Rd5] }) (11... Qb4 { Your queen goes hunting for pawns, but he offers a swap of queens. } 12. Qb3 Qa5 13. Qb5 Qxb5 14. cxb5 { About level, but the queens are gone, and your pawn on e4 is still loose. [%csl Re4] }) (11... Na6 { The knight heads for c5, but it comes one move too late for the pawn on e4. } 12. Nxe4 Bf5 13. Nc3 Nc5 { Slightly better for him: your knight reached c5, but the pawn it was meant to save is gone. [%csl Re4] }) (11... Nd7 { The knight comes out, but the pawn on e4 still has nobody to guard it. } 12. Nxe4 f5 13. Nc3 f4 { Slightly better for him: your pawns roll forward, but you are a pawn down. [%csl Re4] }) (11... Rd8 { The rook stands on the d column, but the pawn on e4 needed your help more. } 12. Nxe4 Bf5 13. Ng3 Bg6 { Slightly better for him: you lost the pawn, and his knight sits safely on g3. [%csl Re4] }) (11... Nc6 { Look again: his pawn on d5 hits c6. [%cal Rd5c6] } 12. dxc6 bxc6 { Much better for him: you gave a whole knight for one pawn. [%csl Rc6] }) (11... Qh4 { The queen guards your e pawn from the side, but his g pawn can chase her away, and then your e pawn falls. } 12. g3 Qe7 13. Nxe4 { Slightly better for him: his g pawn shooed your queen back, and his knight took e4 after all. [%csl Re4] }) (11... Qg5 { The queen hits his g pawn, but his bishop guards it, and his knight takes e4 with a hit on your queen. } 12. Nxe4 Qg6 13. Ng3 { Better for him: he won the pawn on e4, and his knight stands safely on g3. [%csl Rg3] }) 0-1


