{ Your h pawn has just asked his bishop on g5 a question. }
7. Bxf6 { This time he answers by giving up his bishop for your knight. Take it back. } 7... Bxf6 { Your bishop takes back, and now you have two bishops against his one. Against a rook move, prop your d pawn up with the c pawn; against a bishop move or a queen move, hit his centre with the c pawn. } (7... gxf6 { This pawn takes toward the middle, but it tears open the pawns in front of your king. } 8. cxd5 exd5 9. Bd3 f5 10. Qc2 { Better for him: your king has lost its shelter, and his bishop and queen line up against the pawn on f5. [%cal Rd3f5] }) 8. cxd5 { He swaps pawns on d5. Two of your men can take back. Which one is safe? } 8... exd5 { Your e pawn takes back, and your light bishop can see out at last. [%cal Gc8g4] } (8... c5 { The c pawn hits back instead of taking, and his pawn on d5 slips past it. } 9. dxc6 { His pawn captures yours in passing. } 9... bxc6 10. Bc4 Nd7 11. O-O { Slightly better for him: you are a pawn down, and your pawns on the queen side are split. [%csl Ra7,Rc6] }) (8... c6 { A pawn move in the wrong place: his pawn on d5 simply takes on e6. } 9. dxe6 Bxe6 10. Ne4 Nd7 { Slightly better for him: you are a pawn down, and his knight hits your bishop on f6. [%csl Rf6] }) (8... Qxd5 { Look again: his knight guards d5. [%cal Rc3d5] } 9. Nxd5 { Lost for you: his knight takes your queen. [%cal Rc3d5] }) 9. Qb3 { His queen hits your pawn on d5 and your b pawn too. Against a bishop move or his b pawn to b4, your answer would be the same. What now? } 9... c6 { The c pawn props up d5, and your light bishop still guards b7. You have two bishops against his one, and the game is level. [%csl Gd5,Gb7] } (9... c5 { Just as good: you hit his centre at once, even if it costs a pawn. } 10. Nxd5 cxd4 11. Nxf6+ Qxf6 12. Nxd4 { Level: a pawn down, but your pieces come out fast while his king still stands in the middle. [%csl Ge1] }) (9... Be6 { The bishop guards d5, but it stops guarding b7. [%cal Ge6d5,Rb3b7] } 10. Qxb7 { His queen eats the pawn on b7. } 10... Nd7 11. Nxd5 c5 12. Nxf6+ Nxf6 13. Qa6 { Slightly better for him: two pawns down, though your pieces are active. [%csl Rb7,Rd5] }) (9... Nc6 { The knight comes out, and it leaves d5 short of a guard. } 10. Qxd5 { His queen takes the pawn on d5. } 10... Nb4 11. Qxd8 Rxd8 { Slightly better for him: the queens are off, and he kept the pawn from d5. [%csl Rd5] }) (9... Nd7 { This knight move does nothing for d5, which his knight and queen both hit. } 10. Nxd5 c5 11. Nxf6+ Nxf6 { Better for him: he won the pawn on d5 and swapped off one of your bishops. [%csl Rd5] }) (9... Re8 { The rook slides over to e8, but d5 needed help first. } 10. Nxd5 c5 11. Nxf6+ Qxf6 { Better for him: the pawn on d5 is gone, and so is one of your bishops. [%csl Rd5] }) (9... Bg4 { Your bishop jumps out and hits his knight, but d5 is left to fall. } 10. Nxd5 c5 11. Nxf6+ Qxf6 { Better for him: he took the pawn in the middle, and your bishop on g4 did nothing to stop it. [%csl Rd5] }) (9... Bxd4 { A greedy grab, but his e pawn guards d4. [%cal Re3d4] } 10. exd4 { Much better for him: his pawn took your bishop, and one pawn does not pay for it. [%cal Re3d4] }) (9... Qd6 { Look again: your queen is the only guard of d5, and two of his men hit it. } 10. Nxd5 { Better for him: his knight takes the pawn on d5, and your queen alone could not hold it. [%cal Rc3d5] }) 0-1


