{ Your pawns stand on d5 and e6, and your knight guards the middle. }
4. cxd5 { He swaps pawns in the middle at once. Take back. } 4... exd5 { Your e pawn takes back, and the road in front of your light bishop is open now. [%cal Gc8g4] } (4... Nxd5 { Just as good and very popular: the knight takes back, and he gets to push his king pawn. } 5. e4 Nxc3 6. bxc3 c5 7. Nf3 { About level: he has a big centre, and you hit it from the side with the c pawn. [%csl Rd4,Re4] }) (4... Qxd5 { Look again: his knight on c3 guards d5. [%cal Rc3d5] } 5. Nxd5 exd5 { Lost for you: you traded your queen for a knight. [%cal Rc3d5] }) 5. Bg5 { The pin again: his bishop hits your knight, with your queen behind it. Against his knight to f3 instead, your moves would be the same. [%cal Rg5d8] } 5... c6 { The c pawn props up d5, so your pawn in the middle stands firm. Against his queen to c2 or a knight move next, the same moves follow. [%csl Gd5] } (5... Be7 { Just as good, and the most popular: the bishop first, the c pawn later. } 6. e3 h6 7. Bh4 c6 { About level: this is the very position of our main line, reached by another order. [%csl Gc6] }) (5... Nbd7 { Just as good, though it shuts your light bishop in for now. } 6. e3 Be7 7. Bd3 c6 { Level: a sound set-up, with the light bishop waiting at home. [%csl Bc8] }) (5... Bb4 { Just as good: your bishop pins his knight back, and the game gets sharper. [%cal Gb4e1] } 6. e3 h6 7. Bh4 { About level: a sharper game, with pins on both sides. [%csl Bb4] }) (5... Be6 { About as good: the light bishop comes out to guard d5, though on e6 it has little to aim at. [%cal Ge6d5] } 6. e3 c6 7. Bd3 Be7 { About level: solid, though your bishop on e6 has little to aim at. [%csl Be6] }) (5... h6 { Asking the bishop too early: d5 does not have enough guards yet. } 6. Bxf6 Qxf6 7. Nxd5 { His knight takes the pawn on d5 and hits your queen. } 7... Qd8 8. Nc3 { Better for him: you are a pawn down. The h pawn may ask only when d5 is well guarded. [%csl Rd5] }) (5... Bf5 { The light bishop comes out, but it leaves your pawn on b7 without a guard. } 6. Bxf6 gxf6 7. Qb3 { His queen hits b7 and d5 at once. } 7... c6 8. Qxb7 { Slightly better for him: he won the b pawn, and your king side pawns are split. [%csl Rb7] }) (5... Nc6 { Just as good: the knight comes out and leans on his d pawn. } 6. e3 Be7 { About level: a fine set-up, though your knight now stands in front of the c pawn. [%csl Bc7] }) (5... c5 { A punch at his centre, but the swap on f6 takes a guard away from d5. } 6. Bxf6 Qxf6 7. Nxd5 Qd8 8. e4 { Slightly better for him: the swap on f6 took a guard off d5, and his knight won the pawn. [%csl Rd5] }) 6. e3 { He builds his little wall with the e pawn. } 6... h6 { The h pawn asks the bishop: take my knight, or step back? } (6... Be7 { Just as good, and the most popular: the bishop first, and the h pawn can ask later. } 7. Bd3 Nbd7 8. Nf3 O-O { About level: a calm set-up, with the question to his bishop saved for later. [%csl Gh7] }) (6... Bf5 { Just as good: your light bishop jumps out on the road the swap opened. } 7. Qf3 { His queen hits your bishop and the pawn on d5. } 7... Bg6 8. Bxf6 Qxf6 9. Qxf6 gxf6 { Level: the queens are off, and your two bishops make up for the doubled pawns. [%csl Gg6] }) (6... Bd6 { Just as good: the bishop takes the d6 square instead. } 7. Bd3 O-O 8. Qc2 h6 9. Bh4 { Level: your bishop eyes his king side from d6, and your king is safe. [%csl Gd6] }) (6... Nbd7 { Just as good, though this knight blocks your light bishop for now. } 7. Bd3 Be7 8. Nf3 O-O { About level: a solid house, with the light bishop still at home. [%csl Bc8] }) (6... Be6 { Just as good: the light bishop comes out to e6 and guards d5. [%cal Ge6d5] } 7. Bd3 Be7 { About level: both your bishops are out, and d5 is well guarded. [%csl Gd5] }) (6... a6 { Just as good: a little pawn move that keeps his knight and bishop away from b5. } 7. Bd3 h6 { About level: you ask the bishop the question one move later. [%csl Bg5] }) (6... a5 { Just as good: the a pawn grabs a little space first. } 7. Bd3 h6 { About level: the question to his bishop comes one move later. [%csl Ga5] }) 7. Bh4 { He keeps his bishop, and the pin stays. } 7... Be7 { The bishop blocks the pin, and your king is one step from castling. If a knight move or a queen move comes next, simply castle. [%csl Ge7] } (7... Bf5 { Just as good: your light bishop comes out first. } 8. Qf3 Be6 9. Bxf6 Qxf6 10. Qxf6 gxf6 { About level: the queens are off, and your pawns on the f column are doubled. [%csl Rf6,Rf7] }) (7... Bd6 { Just as good: the bishop goes to d6 and leaves the pin in place for now. } 8. Bd3 O-O 9. Nge2 Re8 { About level: the pin on your knight is still there. [%csl Rf6] }) (7... Nbd7 { Just as good: the knight first, and the bishop can still come to e7. } 8. Bd3 Be7 9. Bg3 O-O { About level: your king is safe, and the knight on d7 backs up the middle. [%csl Gd7] }) (7... g5 { The g pawn chases his bishop away, but it loosens the pawns around your king. } 8. Bg3 Bf5 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 { Slightly better for him: the light bishops are traded, and the holes around your king remain. [%csl Rf5,Rh5] }) (7... a6 { Just as good: the a pawn steps up before the bishop blocks the pin. } 8. Bd3 Be7 { About level: the bishop blocks the pin one move later. [%csl Ge7] }) (7... a5 { Just as good: the a pawn takes two steps on the queen side. } 8. Bd3 Be7 { About level: the bishop still comes to e7, with your a pawn well ahead. [%csl Ba5] }) 8. Bd3 { His bishop comes out to d3 and looks at your king side. } 8... Ne4 { The knight jumps in, and your bishop on e7 now looks straight at his bishop. He has to trade or step back. [%cal Ge7h4] } (8... O-O { Just as good, and the most popular: castle first, the knight can jump later. } 9. Bg3 c5 10. Nge2 Nc6 { Level: your king is safe, and your c pawn hits his centre. [%csl Gg8] }) (8... Nbd7 { Just as good: the knight comes out before anything else. } 9. h3 O-O 10. Bg3 { Level: a sound game, and the knight on f6 can still jump in later. [%csl Gd7] }) (8... Bg4 { About as good: your bishop hits his queen, though his f pawn chases it back. [%cal Gg4d1] } 9. f3 Be6 10. Nge2 { About level: your bishop went out and came back, while his pawn on f3 guards e4. [%cal Rf3e4] }) (8... Be6 { About as good: the bishop guards d5 once more, though on e6 it will block the e column your rook wants after castling. [%cal Ge6d5] } 9. Nge2 O-O 10. O-O { About level: a solid position, though your bishop is in the way on e6. [%csl Be6] }) (8... c5 { A punch at his centre, but your d pawn is left alone after the swap. } 9. dxc5 Nc6 10. Nge2 Bxc5 { Slightly better for him: your pawn on d5 has no friends beside it, and his pieces can gang up on it. [%csl Rd5] }) (8... a6 { Just as good: a quiet pawn move before the knight jumps in. } 9. Nf3 O-O { About level: you castle first, and the knight jump can wait. [%csl Gg8] }) (8... a5 { Just as good: the a pawn steps up, and your king castles next. } 9. Qc2 O-O { About level: your king is safe, and his queen now guards his bishop on d3. [%cal Bc2d3] }) 9. Bxe7 { He trades the bishops. Take back. } 9... Qxe7 { Your queen takes back. His dark bishop is gone, your knight stands on e4, and your light bishop has an open road. The game is level. [%csl Ge4] } (9... Nxc3 { Just as good: take his knight first. If his bishop takes your queen, your knight takes his queen and your king takes the bishop. } 10. bxc3 Qxe7 { About level: your queen takes back one move later, and his queen side pawns are split. [%csl Ra2,Rc3] }) (9... Kxe7 { The king takes back, but now it can never castle. } 10. Bxe4 { His bishop takes your knight. } 10... dxe4 11. Nge2 Re8 { Better for him: your king is stuck in the middle, and the pawn on e4 is weak. [%csl Re7,Re4] }) 0-1


