{ The queen pawns face each other again. This time, watch where his dark bishop goes. }
2. Bf4 { The London: his dark bishop comes out to f4 before his own pawns can shut it in. Answer as always, with the king knight to f6. [%cal Yg8f6] } 2... Nf6 { The knight guards d5 and keeps every road open. Against his king knight to f3 first, the same knight move. [%csl Gd5] } (2... c5 { Just as good: a punch at his centre straight away. } 3. e3 cxd4 4. exd4 Nc6 { Level, and his king pawn now stands on d4. We bring a piece out first and keep things calm. [%csl Bc5] }) (2... e6 { Just as good: the pawn props up d5 first. } 3. e3 c5 4. Nf3 Nf6 { Level: the same house, built in a different order. [%csl Ge6] }) (2... Bf5 { Also fine: your light bishop gets outside the pawns before they lock it in. } 3. c4 { He hits your d pawn at once. } 3... e6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Nc3 { Level, though his knight on c3 already leans on your d pawn. [%csl Bd5] }) (2... Nc6 { About as good: a natural move, though while the knight sits on c6, your c pawn cannot step up to hit his centre. } 3. e3 Nf6 4. Nf3 { About level: the pawn on c7 is stuck behind your own knight. [%csl Rc7] }) (2... c6 { About as good, if slow: the c pawn props up d5 but hits nothing. } 3. e3 Bf5 4. c4 { About level: he gets to hit your centre first. [%csl Bc6] }) (2... e5 { Look again: his bishop is already looking at e5. } 3. Bxe5 { Slightly better for him: his bishop took the pawn for free. [%cal Rf4e5] }) (2... g6 { Just as good: your dark bishop will come out on g7 instead. } 3. e3 Bg7 { Level: a different house, with your bishop on the long diagonal. [%cal Gg7a1] }) (2... a6 { Just as good: a little waiting move before the knight comes out. } 3. Nf3 e6 { About level: the same house, built one move later. [%csl Ge6] }) 3. e3 { He props up his centre with the king pawn. Do the same: your king pawn to e6 props up d5 and opens a road for your dark bishop. [%cal Ye7e6] } 3... e6 { Your pawns on d5 and e6 stand together like a wall, and your dark bishop can come out. [%csl Gd5,Ge6] } (3... c5 { Just as good, and very popular: the c pawn hits his centre first. } 4. Nd2 Nc6 5. Ngf3 cxd4 6. exd4 { Level: the pawns swapped, and his king pawn now stands on d4. A sharper game than ours. [%csl Bd4] }) (3... Bf5 { Also fine: the light bishop comes out before the e pawn closes its road. } 4. c4 e6 5. Nc3 { Level: a fine game, with his pawn on c4 hitting your centre. [%csl Bf5] }) (3... Nc6 { The knight comes out, but in front of your c pawn. } 4. Nf3 Bg4 { About level: playable, but your c pawn is stuck behind the knight for a long time. [%csl Rc7] }) (3... g6 { Just as good, with a different plan: the dark bishop goes to the corner instead. } 4. Nf3 Bg7 5. c3 { Level: fine chess, but from g7 your bishop looks past his instead of meeting it. [%csl Bg7] }) (3... Bg4 { Your bishop hits his queen at once, and his knight steps in the way. } 4. Nf3 e6 5. Nbd2 { Level: playable, with your bishop pinning his knight to his queen. [%csl Gg4] }) (3... c6 { About as good: the c pawn props up d5, though it gives up the punch at his centre. } 4. Nf3 Bf5 { About level: a sound wall, and slower than our plan. [%csl Bc6] }) (3... e5 { Your e pawn rushes forward, but his bishop on f4 already watches e5. [%cal Rf4e5] } 4. dxe5 Ne4 5. Nd2 Nxd2 6. Qxd2 { Better for him: he kept the extra pawn on e5. [%csl Re5] }) 4. Nf3 { His king knight comes out. Now offer a swap of dark bishops: yours comes out to meet his. Against a knight move to d2, a bishop move or a pawn to c3, the same answer. [%cal Yf8d6] } 4... Bd6 { Your bishop on d6 stares at his on f4. He was proud of that bishop, and now it can be swapped off. [%cal Gd6f4] } (4... c5 { Just as good: the c pawn punches first, and the bishop can come later. } 5. Nbd2 Qb6 6. Rb1 cxd4 7. exd4 { Level: a sharp game, with your queen out early. [%csl Bb6] }) (4... Be7 { Just as good, but it leaves his proud bishop on f4 alone, with a long and happy life ahead. } 5. c4 O-O 6. Nc3 { About level: playable, and his bishop on f4 keeps its fine road. [%csl Bf4] }) (4... Nbd7 { About as good: a useful knight move before the bishop comes out. } 5. c4 Bb4+ 6. Nbd2 { Level: fine, though your bishop went to b4 instead of meeting his. [%csl Bb4] }) (4... Nc6 { About as good, though the knight blocks your c pawn once again. } 5. c4 Bd6 6. Bg3 O-O { About level: your bishop still meets his, but the knight on c6 keeps your c pawn at home. [%csl Rc7] }) (4... Bd7 { Just as good: the light bishop comes out to d7, though it has little to do there. } 5. Be2 c5 { Level: your c pawn punches at his centre anyway. [%csl Gc5] }) (4... a6 { Just as good: the a pawn covers b5 before anything else. [%cal Ga6b5] } 5. c3 c5 { Level: your c pawn hits his centre all the same. [%csl Gd4] }) (4... h6 { Just as good: the h pawn gives your king a little window. } 5. Nbd2 c5 { Level: the c pawn hits his centre next. [%csl Gh6] }) 5. Bg3 { He keeps his bishop and steps back. Your king has waited long enough: castle. Had he swapped on d6, either recapture would be fine; against a knight move, castle too. [%cal Ye8g8] } 5... O-O { Your king is safe in the corner, and the two dark bishops still face each other. [%csl Gg8] } (5... c5 { Just as good: the punch at his centre comes before castling. } 6. dxc5 Bxc5 7. c4 O-O { Level: you castle one move later, and the centre is already open. [%csl Bc5] }) (5... Bxg3 { Just as good: you take his bishop yourself, and his h pawn takes back. } 6. hxg3 { Now his rook on h1 has the h column to look up. } 6... Nbd7 7. c4 { Level, but his rook watches the h column, aimed at the corner where your king wants to castle. [%csl Rh7] }) (5... Ne4 { Just as good: the knight jumps in and hits his bishop on g3, ready to swap itself for that bishop. } 6. Bd3 c5 7. Bxd6 Qxd6 { Level: a pair of bishops came off, and your knight stands on e4. [%csl Ge4] }) (5... Nc6 { About as good, though again the knight steps in front of your c pawn. } 6. c4 O-O 7. Nc3 { About level: your c pawn is stuck, and his pieces press on d5. [%csl Rc7] }) (5... b6 { Just as good: your light bishop heads for b7 before you castle. } 6. c4 O-O 7. Nc3 Bb7 { Level: a calm set-up, with the bishop on the long road. [%csl Gb7] }) (5... Nbd7 { Just as good: a natural knight move before castling. } 6. c4 O-O 7. Nc3 { Level: solid, though the knight on d7 now stands between your queen and your bishop. [%csl Rd7] }) (5... h6 { Just as good: the h pawn steps up first, and the king castles next. } 6. c4 O-O { Level: your king is safe, with an extra window to breathe. [%csl Gh6] }) 6. Bd3 { His light bishop aims at your king's corner. Now the key move: your c pawn punches at his centre pawn. Against a knight move or a pawn to c3 first, the same punch. [%cal Yc7c5] } 6... c5 { That is the pawn break: a pawn move that hits his centre pawn, so lines open for your pieces. Your c pawn now attacks d4. [%cal Gc5d4] } (6... b6 { Just as good: the light bishop gets ready for b7, and the punch comes later. } 7. c3 Bb7 8. Nbd2 { Level: a calm game, and the c pawn can still punch. [%csl Gc7] }) (6... Nbd7 { Solid, but now the knight cuts your queen off from your bishop on d6, so the c pawn has to wait. } 7. Nbd2 b6 8. c3 { Level: playable, but your pieces get in each other's way. [%csl Rd7] }) (6... Nc6 { About as good, though the knight blocks your c pawn once more. } 7. a3 Ne7 8. Nbd2 { About level: the knight had to walk away again, and your c pawn waited. [%csl Re7] }) (6... Ne4 { The knight jumps in, but it needs the f pawn to hold it there. } 7. Nbd2 f5 { About level: your knight is held on e4, and your light bishop is shut in behind your pawns. [%csl Rc8] }) (6... h6 { A little waiting move: the punch can still come. } 7. Nbd2 c5 8. c3 { About level: the same plan, with your extra h pawn step against his extra knight move. [%csl Bh6] }) (6... Bxg3 { Also fine: you swap off his bishop, and his h pawn takes back on g3. } 7. hxg3 c5 8. Ne5 { About level: his rook on the h column and his bishop on d3 both aim at your h pawn. [%csl Rh7] }) (6... e5 { Wrong pawn: his bishop on g3 and his knight both watch e5. [%cal Rg3e5,Rf3e5] } 7. dxe5 Bxe5 8. Nxe5 { Lost for you: his knight took your bishop, and a pawn does not pay for it. [%csl Re5] }) (6... Qe7 { Just as good: the queen steps up, ready to back up the e pawn's push. } 7. Bh4 Nbd7 { About level: a slower plan, aiming for the e pawn's push instead of the c pawn's. [%csl Ge5] }) (6... g6 { Just as good: the g pawn shuts the road his bishop on d3 has toward your king. } 7. Nbd2 c5 { Level: your c pawn hits his centre one move later. [%cal Rd3h7] }) (6... a5 { Just as good: the a pawn grabs a little space. } 7. Qe2 c5 { About level: the c pawn comes next, one move later. [%csl Ga5] }) 7. c3 { He props up d4 with the c pawn. Bring a second attacker: your queen knight to c6. [%cal Yb8c6] } 7... Nc6 { Your pawn break and your knight both hit his pawn on d4, your bishop faces his, and your king is castled. That is how you answer the London: level, with room for every piece. [%csl Gc5,Gc6] } (7... Qc7 { Just as good: the queen guards your bishop from c7, so the bishops can come off on d6 without fuss. } 8. Bxd6 Qxd6 9. Nbd2 { Level: the dark bishops are traded, and your queen stands well on d6. [%csl Gd6] }) (7... Qb6 { Just as good: the queen hits b2 and still guards your bishop along the row. } 8. Qc2 Bd7 9. Nbd2 { Level, with your queen eyeing his queen side. Careful, though: a knight on c6 would cut her guard of your bishop. [%csl Gb6] }) (7... b6 { Just as good: the light bishop gets ready for b7. } 8. Qe2 Bb7 9. Nbd2 { Level: a calm game, though only one of your men hits d4. [%csl Bb6] }) (7... c4 { Just as good, though the pawn rushes past d4, so it no longer hits his centre. } 8. Bc2 b5 9. Nbd2 { Level, but the pressure on d4 is gone, and his king pawn is free to push up. [%csl Rc4] }) (7... cxd4 { About as good, though you swap pawns at once and let the tension go. } 8. exd4 Nc6 9. Nbd2 { About level: the pressure is gone, and his pieces get the e column. [%csl Rc5] }) (7... Nbd7 { Look again: the knight cuts your queen off from your bishop on d6. } 8. Bxd6 { Lost for you: his bishop takes yours, and nothing can take back. [%cal Rg3d6] }) (7... Bxg3 { Just as good, though the swap on g3 hands his rook the h column. } 8. hxg3 Nc6 9. Nbd2 { About level: his rook gets the h column, aimed at your king's corner. [%csl Rh7] }) (7... g6 { Just as good: a careful pawn move that blunts his bishop on d3. } 8. Nbd2 Nc6 { Level: your knight joins the fight a move later. [%csl Gc6] }) 0-1


