5. Ne5 { His knight jumps into e5, right between the two bishops, and blocks yours. You have built this house before. What now? } 5... O-O { Castled, just as in the London you learned: your king is safe before the middle opens. [%csl Gg8] } (5... Nbd7 { The knight comes to challenge e5, but it cuts your queen off from your bishop on d6. } 6. Bd3 Ne4 7. Bxe4 dxe4 8. Nd2 { Slightly better for him: nothing guards your pawn on e4, and his knight on d2 is already hitting it. [%csl Re4] }) (5... Bxe5 { You take his knight, but his d pawn takes back and hits your knight on f6. } 6. dxe5 Ne4 7. f3 Nc5 { Slightly better for him: his pawn on e5 cramps you, and your dark bishop is gone. [%csl Re5] }) (5... c5 { About as good: the punch comes at once, though now his bishop can check on b5, and your c pawn can no longer step in the way. [%cal Rb5e8] } 6. Bb5+ { Check! A block on d7 would cut your queen off from your bishop on d6, so your king has to step aside. [%csl Rd6] } 6... Ke7 7. c3 { About level, but your king had to step up to e7 and can never castle. [%csl Re7] }) (5... Nc6 { Just as good: the knight comes out and offers to swap his knight on e5. } 6. Nd2 O-O 7. c3 { Level, but your c pawn sits behind the knight. [%csl Rc7] }) (5... c6 { Solid: your c pawn props up d5, but it hits nothing, so he gets his own c pawn forward first. [%csl Bc6] } 6. c4 O-O 7. Nc3 { Level: playable, but he got his pawn to c4 first. [%csl Bc6] }) (5... Qe7 { About as good: your queen guards the bishop on d6 from the side, though your c pawn has not hit his centre yet. [%csl Bc7] } 6. Nc3 O-O 7. h4 { About level: his h pawn marches at your king while you wait. [%csl Rh4] }) (5... a6 { Just as good: a small pawn move, and your king can castle soon. } 6. c4 c5 { Level: your pawn break comes at once, right against his centre. [%csl Gc5] }) (5... Nfd7 { Just as good: the knight steps back to challenge his knight on e5. } 6. Nc3 Nc6 { Level: your two knights crowd his knight on e5. [%cal Gd7e5,Gc6e