4. Nf3 { This time he brings his king knight out first and keeps his dark bishop at home. What now? } 4... Be7 { The bishop comes out to e7 and guards your knight. If his bishop goes to g5 now, it is the main line again. } (4... Bb4 { Just as good: your bishop pins his knight on c3. A sharper opening of its own. } 5. Bg5 O-O 6. e3 { Level: two pins on the board, and a sharper game than ours. [%csl Gb4] }) (4... c6 { Just as good: the c pawn props up d5, a solid wall. } 5. e3 Nbd7 6. Qc2 { Level: a sound wall, with your light bishop shut in behind it. [%csl Bc8] }) (4... c5 { Just as good: the c pawn strikes at once, before your bishop comes out. } 5. cxd5 cxd4 6. Qxd4 exd5 { Level: the middle is wide open, and his queen stands in the centre. [%csl Bd4] }) (4... Nbd7 { Just as good: the queen knight first, a flexible move. } 5. Qc2 c6 6. e3 { Level: solid, with your bishop still choosing its square. [%csl Bf8] }) (4... dxc4 { Just as good: you take the c pawn, and he grabs the middle with his king pawn. } 5. e4 Bb4 6. Bxc4 { Level: his bishop took back on c4, and his pawns stand broad in the middle. [%csl Bc4] }) 5. Bf4 { His bishop comes to f4 this time, and it pins nothing. Against the pawn to e3 instead, your answer would be the same. What now? } 5... O-O { Your king is safe in the corner, and your rook joins the game. [%csl Gg8] } (5... c6 { About as good: solid, though your king waits in the middle. } 6. e3 Nbd7 7. Qc2 O-O { About level: a sound wall, castled one move later. [%csl Bc6] }) (5... a6 { Just as good: a little pawn move to keep his pieces off b5. } 6. e3 dxc4 7. Bxc4 b5 8. Be2 { About level: your queen side pawns moved up, and your king still waits. [%csl Re8] }) (5... c5 { Just as good: the punch before castling, a sharp try. } 6. dxc5 O-O 7. e3 Bxc5 { Level, though your bishop spent two moves to reach c5. [%csl Bc5] }) (5... Nbd7 { About as good: a natural knight move, though now his knight can jump to b5. } 6. Nb5 { His knight and bishop both hit your pawn on c7, which only your queen guards. } 6... Bb4+ 7. Nd2 e5 { About level: your e pawn blocks his bishop's road to c7, and if he takes it, your knight jumps to h5 to hit his bishop. [%csl Gc7] }) (5... dxc4 { Just as good, though taking on c4 now hands his light bishop a free move when it takes back. } 6. e3 O-O 7. Bxc4 c5 8. O-O { About level: his bishop came out with a capture, for free. [%csl Bc4] }) (5... b6 { Just as good: your light bishop heads for b7, and castling can wait a move. } 6. Qc2 Bb7 7. e3 { About level: a fine plan, with your king still in the middle. [%csl Re8] }) (5... Nc6 { The knight comes out, but it stands in front of your c pawn, so that pawn cannot hit his centre while it stays. } 6. e3 Ne4 7. Bd3 Nxc3 8. bxc3 { About level: his pieces stand well, and your knight on c6 still blocks your c pawn. [%csl Rc7] }) 6. e3 { He builds his little wall, and his king still stands in the middle, while yours is safe. What now? } 6... c5 { That is the pawn break: your c pawn hits his d pawn, and the middle opens while your king is already safe. Against a bishop move, your c pawn takes on d4. [%cal Gc5d4] } (6... b6 { Just as good: the light bishop heads for b7 first. } 7. Bd3 dxc4 8. Bxc4 Bb7 9. O-O { About level: a calm game, and your c pawn can still punch later. [%csl Gb7] }) (6... a6 { Just as good: a small waiting move, and he swaps in the middle. } 7. cxd5 Nxd5 8. Nxd5 exd5 9. Bd3 { About level: a calm game, but you never hit his centre. [%csl Bc7] }) (6... Nbd7 { Just as good and natural, though he grabs space with his c pawn. } 7. c5 Nh5 8. Bd3 c6 { Level: his pawn on c5 takes space, and your knight went to the rim to chase his bishop. [%csl Rc5] }) (6... c6 { Just as good, a solid wall: your c pawn props up d5, though it only goes one square and hits nothing in his centre. [%csl Bc6] } 7. Qc2 Nbd7 8. a3 { About level: a sound wall, but no pressure on his centre. [%csl Bc6] }) (6... dxc4 { Just as good, though you take on c4 at once, and his bishop takes back with a free move. } 7. Bxc4 c5 8. dxc5 Qxd1+ 9. Rxd1 Bxc5 { About level: the queens came off, and his rook owns the d column. [%csl Rd1] }) (6... e5 { The wrong pawn break: his bishop on f4 and his d pawn both hit e5. [%cal Rf4e5,Rd4e5] } 7. dxe5 Ne4 8. cxd5 Nxc3 9. bxc3 { Much better for him: he is two pawns up. [%csl Re5,Rd5] }) (6... h6 { Just as good: the h pawn steps up and gives your king a window. } 7. Qc2 c5 { About level: you still hit his centre with the c pawn, a move later. [%csl Gc5] }) 7. cxd5 { He swaps on d5, a small mistake: taking on c5 was his better move. Now the middle opens with your king castled and his still at home. How do you take back? } 7... Nxd5 { The knight takes back and hits his knight and his bishop at once. If his bishop steps back to g3 instead of trading, take on d4. [%csl Gc3,Gf4] } (7... exd5 { Natural, but after he takes on c5, your d pawn will have no pawn beside it. } 8. dxc5 Bxc5 9. Bb5 { About level: your pawn on d5 stands alone, and his pieces can gang up on it. [%csl Rd5] }) (7... cxd4 { Just as good: the c pawn takes first, and his d pawn jumps forward. } 8. d6 Bxd6 9. Bxd6 Qxd6 10. Qxd4 { Level: the dark bishops are traded, and the queens face each other. [%csl Gd6] }) (7... Qb6 { Your queen hits b2, but his pawn on d5 is still alive. } 8. dxe6 Bxe6 9. a3 { About level: he won a pawn in the middle, but your pieces are out first and his king is still at home. [%csl Re6] }) (7... Qxd5 { Look again: the knight on c3 still guards d5. [%cal Rc3d5] } 8. Nxd5 { Lost for you: his knight takes your queen, and taking it back gets you only a knight. [%cal Rc3d5] }) (7... Nc6 { Look again: his pawn on d5 attacks c6. } 8. dxc6 { Lost for you: his pawn takes your knight, and taking back on c6 wins only a pawn for it. [%cal Rd5c6] }) 8. Nxd5 { He swaps knights on d5. How do you take back this time? } 8... Qxd5 { The queen takes back and stands in the middle, where nothing of his attacks her yet. Against a bishop move or his pawn to a3, your c pawn takes on d4. } (8... exd5 { Just as good: the pawn takes back and keeps the queens at home. } 9. dxc5 Bxc5 10. a3 Bb6 11. Bd3 { Level, though your pawn on d5 stands alone in the middle. [%csl Bd5] }) (8... cxd4 { Look again: his knight on d5 is still there, and it can take your bishop on e7 with check. } 9. Nxe7+ { His knight takes your bishop with check. } 9... Qxe7 10. exd4 { Lost for you: his knight took your knight and then your bishop, and you got only one knight back. [%csl Re7] }) (8... Nc6 { Look again: his knight on d5 can take your bishop with check. } 9. Nxe7+ Qxe7 10. Be2 { Lost for you: you are a whole piece down. [%csl Re7] }) (8... Bd6 { Your bishop offers a swap, but you forgot his knight on d5. } 9. Bxd6 Qxd6 10. Nc3 { Lost for you: his knight escaped, and you are a piece down. [%csl Rc3] }) 9. dxc5 { He takes your c pawn, and your queen faces his down the d column. How do you take back? } 9... Qxc5 { Your queen takes back and stays on the board. Material is level, you are castled, and his king still stands in the middle with his light bishop at home. [%csl Gg8,Re1] } (9... Qxd1+ { About as good: the queens come off, and his rook takes back. } 10. Rxd1 Bxc5 11. a3 { About level: without queens his king is safe in the middle, and his rook holds the d column. [%csl Rd1] }) (9... Bxc5 { The bishop takes back, but his queen swaps yours off. } 10. Qxd5 exd5 11. Rc1 Bb4+ 12. Kd1 { About level: your d pawn stands alone, and his rook owns the c column. [%csl Rd5] }) (9... Nc6 { You bring a piece out instead, and his queen swaps yours off. } 10. Qxd5 exd5 11. Bb5 { About level: he keeps the pawn on c5 for now, and your d pawn stands alone. [%csl Rc5] }) (9... Nd7 { The knight wants to take on c5, but his queen trades first. } 10. Qxd5 exd5 11. Bd6 { About level: his bishop guards the pawn on c5 for now. [%csl Rd6] }) (9... Qxf3 { Look again: his queen and his g pawn both guard the knight on f3. [%cal Rd1f3,Rg2f3] } 10. Qx