n crashes forward before your king has castled. } 8... Be7 9. Ne5 Nxe5 10. dxe5 { Slightly better for him: his pawn on e5 kicked your knight, and your king is still in the middle. [%csl Re5] }) (7... cxd4 { About as good: you swap the pawns, and his e pawn takes back. } 8. exd4 O-O 9. Re1 Qc7 10. Nf1 { About level: your pawn break is used up, and his dark bishop has an open road. [%csl Rc1] }) (7... Bxh2+ { Look again: the bishop takes on h2 with check, but his knight on f3 guards that square. } 8. Nxh2 { Much better for him: you gave your bishop for a single pawn. [%cal Rf3h2] }) (7... a5 { The a pawn grabs space, but your king still waits in the middle. } 8. Re1 h6 9. a3 { Level: his king pawn is ready to go, and your king has not castled. [%csl Re8] }) 8. dxc5 { He takes your c pawn, so your bishop must move again: take it back. Against a rook move or a queen move, your e pawn could punch at once; against his pawn to e4, you would take on d4. } 8... Bxc5 { Your bishop takes back, and material is level again. If his b pawn comes to b4 and hits it, the bishop steps back to d6. [%csl Gc5] } (8... Be7 { You leave his pawn standing on c5. } 9. b4 { His b pawn props up the pawn on c5. } 9... a5 10. Nd4 { Slightly better for him: he keeps the extra pawn on c5. [%csl Rc5] }) (8... Ne5 { Your knight jumps forward instead of taking back. } 9. Nxe5 Bxe5 10. Qe2 { Slightly better for him: the knights are swapped, but he is still a pawn up on c5. [%csl Rc5] }) 9. e4 { There it is: his king pawn hits your d pawn, the push his whole set-up was built for. What now? } 9... Qc7 { Your queen steps to c7 and keeps the tension in the middle. The queen watches e5, your king is safe, and you are level. [%csl Ge5] } (9... dxe4 { The natural capture, but it hands him the middle. } 10. Nxe4 Nxe4 11. Bxe4 { His bishop lands on e4, aiming at your king and at the knight on c6. } 11... Qc7 12. Qe2 { About level, but his bishop rules the middle and your light bishop is still at home. [%csl Re4