{ Pawn against pawn in the middle. Not everybody likes a quiet game, and some players will throw pawns at you. }
2. e4 { He offers a pawn: the Blackmar-Diemer gambit, named after the two players who made it famous. He hopes to open lines and race his pieces out. What now? } 2... dxe4 { Take it: you are a pawn up, and your pawn on e4 stands in his way. Against the pawn to f3, take again and bring out the knight and the e pawn. [%csl Ge4] } (2... c6 { Playable: you prop up d5 instead of taking. } 3. e5 Bf5 4. h4 h5 5. c4 { Level: the Caro-Kann, another opening, and the pawn he offered is still on the board. [%csl Be5] }) (2... e6 { Playable: you decline the pawn and go into the French. } 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 dxe4 5. Nxe4 { Level: the French, a different opening, with his pieces active. [%csl Be6] }) (2... Nc6 { The knight comes out, but now his pawn pushes past yours. } 3. e5 Bf5 4. c3 e6 5. Nd2 { About level: his pawns on d4 and e5 take space, and your knight blocks your c pawn. [%csl Re5] }) (2... Nf6 { The knight hits his pawn, but the pawn just pushes on and kicks it. } 3. e5 Nfd7 { Slightly better for him: your knight was pushed back, and his pawns own the middle. [%csl Re5] }) (2... c5 { A counter punch, but his centre wins the race. } 3. dxc5 Nf6 4. exd5 Nxd5 5. Bc4 { Slightly better for him: you are a pawn down, and his bishop hits your knight. [%csl Rd5] }) (2... e5 { A punch of your own, but the queens come off and his pawns are better. } 3. dxe5 dxe4 4. Qxd8+ Kxd8 5. Nc3 { Better for him: your king can no longer castle, and your pawn on e4 is loose. [%csl Re4] }) (2... Bf5 { Look again: his e pawn takes your bishop on f5. } 3. exf5 { Lost for you: a whole bishop gone for nothing. [%cal Re4f5] }) 3. Nc3 { His knight attacks your pawn on e4. What now? } 3... Nf6 { Your knight guards the pawn and comes out at the same time. Against a bishop move to g5, your bishop goes to f5 to hold the pawn. } (3... e5 { You give the pawn back to free your pieces. } 4. Nge2 Nc6 5. Be3 Bb4 6. dxe5 { Level: he took on e5, and the material is even again. [%csl Be5] }) (3... f5 { The f pawn props up e4, but it opens your king a little. } 4. f3 e5 5. dxe5 Qxd1+ 6. Kxd1 { Level: the queens are gone, and so is your extra pawn. [%csl Re4] }) (3... Bf5 { Your bishop guards e4, but his pawns can chase it. [%cal Gf5e4] } 4. g4 { His g pawn hits your bishop. } 4... Bg6 5. Nge2 e5 6. Be3 { Level: you are still a pawn up, but his pieces are flying out. [%csl Rg6] }) (3... c5 { A counter punch at his centre. } 4. Nge2 Nf6 5. d5 e6 6. Nf4 { Level: his pawn on d5 is strong, and the game is sharp. [%csl Rd5] }) (3... Nc6 { This knight hits his centre, but his pawn just pushes on. } 4. d5 Nb8 5. Bg5 h6 6. Bh4 { Level: your knight went back home, and his pieces are out. [%csl Rb8] }) (3... Bg4 { Look again: your bishop hits his queen, but she can take it straight away. } 4. Qxg4 { Lost for you: his queen ate your bishop, and your pawn on e4 is loose too. [%cal Rd1g4] }) 4. f3 { Here is the real gambit: he offers a second pawn, to open the f column for his rook and race his pieces out. What now? } 4... exf3 { You take the second pawn too, and his king side is wide open. } (4... Bf5 { Just as good: your bishop props up e4 instead of taking. } 5. fxe4 Nxe4 6. Qf3 { His queen hits your bishop on f5 and your knight on e4. } 6... Nxc3 7. bxc3 e6 { About level: you are a pawn up for now, but your pawn on b7 is loose to his queen. [%csl Rb7] }) (4... c5 { Just as good: you hit his centre while the pawn on e4 stays. } 5. d5 exf3 6. Nxf3 g6 7. Be2 { About level, and a pawn up for you, though his pawn on d5 is a thorn. [%csl Rd5] }) (4... c6 { Also fine and solid: you let him take the pawn on e4 back and keep a compact position. } 5. Nxe4 Nbd7 6. Nxf6+ Nxf6 { Level: he won his pawn back, and the material is even. [%csl Ge4] }) (4... Nc6 { The knight hits his centre. } 5. d5 Ne5 6. fxe4 e6 { Level: he took his pawn back, and the middle is open. [%csl Re4] }) (4... e3 { You give the pawn back to slow him down. } 5. Bxe3 e6 6. Bd3 Be7 { About level: the extra pawn is gone, and his bishops point at your king. [%csl Re3] }) (4... e5 { A punch in the middle, but his pawn simply takes it. } 5. dxe5 Nfd7 { Your knight steps back, and his pawn on e5 is strong. } 6. f4 { Slightly better for him: your extra pawn is gone, and his pawn on e5 cramps you. [%csl Re5] }) 5. Nxf3 { His knight takes back. He is a pawn down, but his pieces come out fast. What now? } 5... e6 { The pawn on e6 builds your house again and frees your dark bishop, and you keep the extra pawn. Had his queen taken back on f3, yours could have taken his d pawn. [%csl Ge6] } (5... c6 { Just as good: the c pawn takes away squares from his pieces. } 6. Bc4 Bf5 7. Ne5 e6 { Slightly better for you: a pawn up, and your bishop got out before the e pawn moved. [%csl Gf5] }) (5... Bf5 { Just as good: the light bishop gets out before the pawns close its road. } 6. Bd3 Bxd3 7. Qxd3 c6 { About level, and a pawn up for you: the light bishops are swapped. [%csl Gc6] }) (5... g6 { Just as good: your bishop goes to g7. [%cal Gf8g7] } 6. Bf4 c6 7. Qd2 Bg7 { About level, and a pawn up for you, with your bishop on g7. [%csl Gg7] }) (5... Bg4 { Just as good: your bishop pins his knight to his queen. } 6. h3 Bh5 7. g4 Bg6 8. Ne5 { Level: his pawns chased your bishop, but you are still a pawn up. [%csl Rg6] }) (5... a6 { Just as good: a little pawn move that keeps his pieces off b5. } 6. Bg5 g6 7. Qd2 Bg7 { About level, and a pawn up for you. [%csl Gb5] }) (5... Nc6 { About as good: the knight hits his d pawn, though the pawn steps forward. } 6. a3 g6 7. d5 Nb8 { Level: you are a pawn up, but your knight went home. [%csl Rb8] }) (5... Qxd4 { Look again: his knight on f3 guards the d pawn. } 6. Nxd4 { Lost for you: you gave your queen for a pawn. [%cal Rf3d4] }) (5... Be6 { Just as good: the light bishop comes out to e6, though it now stands in front of your e pawn. } 6. Be3 c6 7. Bd3 { About level: still a pawn up, and his pieces are out and lively. [%csl Ge6] }) (5... c5 { The pawn break at once, but he takes it, and the queens come off. } 6. dxc5 Qxd1+ 7. Nxd1 e6 { Level: he won his pawn back, and with the queens gone his gambit has cost him nothing. [%csl Rc5] }) 6. Bg5 { His bishop pins your knight to your queen. Against a bishop move to d3 or c4, your answer would be the same. What now? } 6... Be7 { The bishop blocks the pin, and your king is one step from castling. [%csl Ge7] } (6... a6 { Just as good: a little pawn move while the pin stays on. } 7. Bd3 Be7 8. Qe2 Nc6 { Level: you blocked the pin a move later, and his pieces kept coming. [%csl Ra6] }) (6... h6 { About as good: you ask the bishop, and he takes your knight. } 7. Bxf6 gxf6 8. Qd2 Nd7 { Level: you keep the extra pawn, but the pawns in front of your king are broken. [%csl Rf6] }) (6... Nc6 { The knight hits his d pawn while the pin is still on. } 7. Qd2 h6 8. Bh4 Be7 { Level: your bishop still reaches e7 in the end, and his queen side is ready to castle. [%csl Ge7] }) (6... Nbd7 { The other knight backs up the pinned one. } 7. Bd3 h6 8. Bh4 c5 { Level: a fine position, just a touch slower than ours. [%csl Gd7] }) (6... Bb4 { A pin of your own on his knight. } 7. Bd3 h6 8. Bxf6 Qxf6 9. O-O { Level: the pins cancel out, and he is happy to castle. [%csl Bb4] }) (6... c5 { The pawn break is too early: his bishop takes your knight and checks. } 7. Bxf6 Qxf6 8. Bb5+ { Check, and your king has to deal with it. } 8... Bd7 9. Ne5 { About level: your bishop on d7 is pinned and hit twice, and his d pawn can take your extra pawn on c5. [%csl Rd7] }) (6... Bd6 { Your bishop comes to d6 instead, and the pin on your knight stays. } 7. Qd2 Nbd7 8. O-O-O { Level: still a pawn up, but his king is safe and his pieces aim at yours. [%cal Rg5f6] }) 7. Bd3 { His bishop aims at your king side. What now? } 7... Nc6 { You are a pawn up, your king can castle next, and your knight and queen both look at his d pawn. Had his queen gone to d2, your c pawn would have punched at once. [%csl Gd4] } (7... O-O { Just as good: your king is safe at once. } 8. Qe2 Nc6 { About level, and a pawn up for you: your king is safe and your knight is out. [%csl Gg8] }) (7... Na6 { Just as good: the knight heads for b4 to chase his bishop. } 8. Qe2 Nb4 9. Bc4 O-O { About level, and you are still a pawn up: your knight chased his bishop off d3. [%csl Gb4] }) (7... a6 { A small pawn move to keep his pieces off b5. } 8. Bxf6 Bxf6 9. Ne4 Nd7 { About level, and a pawn up for you, with the two bishops. [%csl Gf6] }) (7... h6 { You ask the bishop, but he can win his pawn back. } 8. Bxf6 Bxf6 9. Ne4 c5 10. dxc5 { Level: he won his pawn back, so the extra pawn is gone. [%csl Rc5] }) (7... Nbd7 { A calm knight move, but it does not hit his d pawn. } 8. Qe2 a6 9. O-O-O b5 { Level: you are still a pawn up, but he castled the long way and his pieces aim at your king. [%csl Rc1] }) (7... c5 { The pawn break, but his d pawn simply takes it. } 8. dxc5 h6 9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. Qe2 { Level: your extra pawn is gone and the pawns around your king are broken. [%csl Rf6] }) (7... Nd5 { Your knight jumps in to trade, but it lets him catch up. } 8. Bxe7 Qxe7 9. Qd2 Nc6 { Level: you are a pawn up, but after the trade his queen came out, and his king is ready to castle. [%csl Gd5] }) (7... e5 { A punch in the middle, but his d pawn simply takes it and kicks your knight. } 8. dxe5 Nh5 9. Be3 { Better for him: your extra pawn is gone, and your knight sits on the rim. [%csl Rh5] }) 0-1


