{ Your e pawn has just stepped up to hit his centre, and your other pawn still stands on e4. }
11. Nxe4 { He grabs the pawn on e4. It looks free, and it is a mistake. What now? [%csl Re4] } 11... exd4 { Your pawn takes on d4, and your queen looks straight down the e column at his knight. Pushing his d pawn past yours, out of reach, was his move. [%cal Ge7e4] } (11... Bf5 { The bishop hits his knight, but the knight simply steps away with the pawn it won. } 12. Ng3 Bg6 13. Be2 { Slightly better for him: he is a pawn up, and his king is safe. [%csl Re4] }) (11... c6 { A solid pawn move, but it leaves his knight on e4 with the pawn it won. } 12. d5 cxd5 13. cxd5 { About level, but he is a pawn up, and his pawn on d5 stands in the middle. [%csl Rd5] }) (11... Nc6 { The knight hits his d pawn, but the pawn walks past it and chases the knight. } 12. d5 Nb4 13. a3 { Better for him: your knight is kicked away, and he is a pawn up. [%csl Rb4] }) (11... Qb4+ { A check, but his queen blocks it and the queens come off. } 12. Qd2 Qxd2+ 13. Kxd2 { Better for him: the queens are gone, and he is still a pawn up. [%csl Re4] }) (11... Rd8 { The rook lines up against his d pawn, but the pawn walks past it. } 12. d5 Bf5 13. Ng3 { Slightly better for him: he is a pawn up, and his pawn on d5 cramps you. [%csl Rd5] }) (11... Bg4 { The bishop goes after his queen, but nothing guards it on g4. } 12. Qxg4 f5 { Your f pawn hits the queen and the knight at once. } 13. Qg3 fxe4 { Slightly better for him: you won the knight back for your bishop, but you are still a pawn down. [%csl Re4] }) (11... f5 { The f pawn kicks his knight, but the knight can simply step back. } 12. Nc3 { Slightly better for him: his knight is safe on c3, and he keeps the pawn it won. [%csl Rc3] }) 12. Qxd4 { He takes the pawn back. That is the natural move, and it is the second mistake. Your move. [%csl Re4] } 12... Rd8 { The rook hits his queen, the only guard of his knight on e4, so she must leave the d column. His bishop to d3, guarding the knight, was his move. [%cal Gd8d4,Ge7e4] } (12... Nc6 { Just as good: the knight hits his queen, and she is the only guard of his knight on e4. } 13. Nf6+ gxf6 14. Qc3 { Much better for you: you won his knight for a pawn. [%csl Gf6] }) (12... Qxe4 { Look again: his queen guards the knight on e4. } 13. Qxe4 { Lost for you: his queen takes yours. [%cal Rd4e4] }) (12... c5 { The pawn hits his queen, but she finds a square that still guards the knight. } 13. Qd3 Bf5 14. f3 { Better for you: his knight on e4 is pinned to his queen on d3, and two of your pieces hit it. [%cal Gf5d3] }) (12... Bf5 { The bishop hits the knight too, but the knight just steps away to safety. } 13. Ng3 Be6 14. Qc3 { Slightly better for him: his knight escaped, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%csl Rg3] }) (12... Re8 { The rook joins the e column, but your own queen stands between it and the knight. } 13. Ng3 Nc6 14. Qc3 { Slightly better for him: the knight stepped back to safety, and the extra pawn is still his. [%csl Re7] }) 13. Qc5 { His queen steps off the d column, hits yours and offers a trade. What now? } 13... Qxe4 { Your queen takes the knight and dodges his queen at once: a knight up for a pawn. His best try was a knight check on f6, to break up your pawns. [%csl Ge4] } (13... Qh4 { The queen steps away from his and still eyes the knight on e4, but it is his move, and the knight just walks away. } 14. Ng3 Qf6 15. Qa3 { About level: his knight got away, and he keeps the extra pawn. [%csl Rg3] }) (13... Qe8 { The queen steps back to safety, and his knight escapes as well. } 14. Ng3 Na6 15. Qa3 { About level: his knight is safe on g3, and the extra pawn is his. [%csl Rg3] }) (13... Qd7 { The queen steps aside to d7, and his knight is left in peace. } 14. Be2 Na6 15. Qe5 { Slightly better for him: his knight stays, and he is a pawn up. [%csl Re4] }) (13... Qxc5 { The queens come off, but his knight takes back on c5 and is safe again. } 14. Nxc5 { Better for him: the queens are gone, his knight escaped, and he is a pawn up. [%csl Rc5] }) 0-1


