{ Your queen stands on c7, and his king pawn faces your d pawn in the middle. }
10. e5 { His pawn pushes on and hits your knight. It looks strong, and it is a mistake. What now? [%csl Rf6] } 10... Nxe5 { Your knight takes the pawn instead of running away, and your queen backs it up. With his queen on e2 first, that pawn would have had a second guard. [%cal Gc7e5] } (10... Nd7 { The knight runs to safety, but now his bishop crashes in on h7. } 11. Bxh7+ Kxh7 12. Ng5+ Kg6 13. b4 { Level: you are a bishop up for a pawn, but your king stands out on g6 with his pieces all around it. [%csl Rg6] }) (10... Ng4 { The knight jumps forward, but his bishop takes on h7 with check first. } 11. Bxh7+ Kxh7 12. Ng5+ Kg8 13. Qxg4 { About level: he got your knight back, and your king has lost the pawn in front of it. [%csl Rh7] }) (10... Ne4 { The knight jumps into the middle, but he swaps it off and takes a pawn. } 11. Nxe4 dxe4 12. Bxe4 { About level: he took your d pawn, but his pawn on e5 has one guard against your two attackers. [%csl Ge5] }) (10... Qxe5 { Look again: his knight guards the pawn on e5. } 11. Nxe5 Nxe5 { Lost for you: you gave your queen for a knight and a pawn. [%csl Re5] }) (10... Ne8 { The knight steps all the way back, and his pawn on e5 stays. } 11. Nb3 Bb6 12. Bf4 { Slightly better for him: his pawn on e5 cramps you, and your knight on e8 has no good square. [%csl Re5] }) (10... Bxf2+ { First a check on f2 to grab a pawn, but his rook takes your bishop. } 11. Rxf2 Nxe5 12. Nxe5 Qxe5 { Much better for him: his rook stands on f2, and a bishop is worth far more than your two pawns. [%csl Rf2] }) 11. Nxe5 { His knight takes back. Take it back. } 11... Qxe5 { Your queen takes the knight, and the pawn from e5 is yours. [%csl Ge5] } 12. Nf3 { His other knight hits your queen. Where should she go? [%csl Re5] } 12... Qc7 { Back to c7, safe from his pieces, and you are a clean pawn up: the pawn that pushed to e5 is gone. [%csl Gc7] } (12... Qd6 { A fine square, but his knight and bishop can hit the queen there again. } 13. Qe2 h6 14. b4 { Slightly better for you: still a pawn up, but your queen on d6 is in the way of your own pieces. [%csl Gd6] }) (12... Qh5 { The queen heads for his king, but she can be chased about. } 13. Bf4 Ne4 14. Qc2 { About level: you keep the extra pawn, but his pieces come out with threats. [%csl Rh5] }) (12... Qb8 { Your queen is safe in the corner, but far from the fight. } 13. Re1 Bd6 14. Bg5 { Slightly better for you: a pawn up, though your queen on b8 is out of play. [%csl Rb8] }) (12... Bxf2+ { A check that grabs a pawn, but it just gives him the bishop. } 13. Rxf2 Qh5 14. Qe1 { Much better for him: you have two pawns for a whole bishop. [%csl Rf2] }) (12... Qxh2+ { Look again: his king and his knight both guard h2. [%cal Rg1h2,Rf3h2] } 13. Kxh2 { Lost for you: your queen is gone for a single pawn. [%csl Rh2] }) 0-1


