8. O-O { He castles. It is the natural move, and it is a mistake: a little pawn step to a3 was better. What now? } 8... Nxd4 { Your knight takes his d pawn. If his knight takes back, your queen takes back with check. [%cal Gd8d4] } (8... O-O { Castling is natural and fine, but it lets the free pawn on d4 go for now. } 9. Ne4 Nxd4 10. Nxf6+ Bxf6 11. Bxf6 Qxf6 { Slightly better for you: two pawns up after a pile of trades, but his bishop stares at your king. [%csl Rh7] }) (8... h6 { Asking his bishop is sensible, but the pawn on d4 was free right now. } 9. Bxf6 Bxf6 10. Bb5 { Slightly better for you: a pawn up, but his bishop pins your knight to your king. [%cal Rb5e8] }) (8... a6 { A small pawn move, and the chance on d4 is gone. } 9. Kh1 Nb4 10. Bc4 { About level: still a pawn up, but all his pieces are out. [%csl Gb4] }) (8... Nd5 { The knight offers trades, but he swaps it off and his d pawn is safe again. } 9. Nxd5 exd5 10. Qe1 { About level: a pawn up, but his pieces are lively and your king is still in the middle. [%csl Re8] }) (8... Bd7 { The bishop comes out, but now your queen can no longer reach d4. } 9. a3 O-O 10. Qd2 { Level: still a pawn up, but the free pawn on d4 got away. [%csl Rd7] }) (8... Qxd4+ { The queen grabs the pawn first, but his knight on f3 takes her. } 9. Nxd4 Nxd4 { Lost for you: your queen went for a pawn and a knight. [%cal Rf3d4] }) (8... Nb4 { Your knight chases his bishop, but the pawn on d4 was free right now. } 9. Bc4 a6 10. a3 { About level: still a pawn up, but his bishop got away, and now his queen guards the pawn on d4. [%cal Rd1d4] }) 9. Nxd4 { His knight takes back, the second mistake: the king to h1 was his best. Take it back. } 9... Qxd4+ { Your queen takes back with check. You are two pawns up. [%csl Gd4] } 10. Kh1 { His king steps into the corner. Careful: his bishop on d3 is all that stands between his queen and yours. What now? [%csl Rd3] } 10... Bd7 { The bishop covers b5, so neither his bishop nor his knight can land there safely. Two pawns up, and your queen is safe. [%cal Gd7b5] } (10... Qc5 { The queen steps off the line of his queen, and that works well too. } 11. Qd2 Bd7 12. Be3 { Better for you: two pawns up and safe, just a little slower. [%csl Gc5] }) (10... Qb6 { The queen steps away, but his bishop checks and gains a move. } 11. Bb5+ c6 12. Bd3 { Better for you: two pawns up, though his pieces got a free move. [%csl Gb6] }) (10... c6 { The pawn covers b5, but your queen still stands in front of his. [%cal Gc6b5] } 11. Qe1 Qc5 12. Bf4 { Slightly better for you: two pawns up, but your queen had to run. [%csl Gc5] }) (10... O-O { Look again: his bishop takes on h7 with check, and his queen sees yours. } 11. Bxh7+ Nxh7 12. Qxd4 Bxg5 { Much better for him: the check uncovered his queen and she took yours, and two bishops do not pay for her. [%cal Rd1d4] }) (10... Ng4 { The knight jumps forward, but his bishop checks on b5 and uncovers his queen. } 11. Bb5+ Qd7 12. Bxd7+ Bxd7 { Lost for you: a queen for a bishop, and your knight on g4 is loose as well. [%csl Rg4] }) (10... Qxc3 { Look again: the pawn on b2 guards his knight. } 11. bxc3 { Lost for you: you gave your queen for a knight. [%cal Rb2c3] }) (10... Qd8 { Back home and safe, but your queen gives up her strong square in the middle. } 11. Qf3 { About level: still two pawns up, but his queen and bishop both hit your knight on f6. [%csl Rf6] }) (10... Qg4 { The queen hits his bishop on g5 and offers a swap, but he can guard the bishop and keep the queens on. } 11. Qd2 Bd7 { Better for you: two pawns up, and your bishop now covers b5 too. [%cal Gd7b5] }) (10... Qe5 { The queen steps off his queen's line into the middle, but there his pieces can chase her. } 11. Bf4 Qc5 12. Nb5 { Slightly better for you: two pawns up, but his knight and bishop both hit your pawn on c7. [%csl Rc7] }) (10... h6 { Look again: his bishop on d3 can step away with a check on b5. } 11. Bb5+ c6 12. Qxd4 { Lost for you: the check uncovered his queen, and she took yours. [%cal Rd1d4] }) (10... b6 { Look again: your queen still stands on his queen's line, and his bishop can step off it with a check. } 11. Bb5+ Qd7 12. Bxd7+ Bxd7 { Lost for you: your queen blocked the check, and you got only his bishop for her. [%csl Rd7] }) 0-1


