{ Your queen stands in the middle on d5, and your c pawn still hits his d pawn. }
9. b3 { He pushes his b pawn one square. A quiet move, and a mistake. What now? } 9... cxd4 { Your c pawn takes on d4. If his e pawn takes back, your queen checks on e4 and hits his bishop too. Taking your c pawn first was his move. [%cal Gd5e4] } (9... Nc6 { Natural development, but the chance on d4 slips away. } 10. Bc4 Qf5 11. Bd3 Qd5 { Level: his bishop chased your queen to and fro, and his centre still stands. [%csl Rd4] }) (9... Qd8 { The queen steps back before anything has happened, and the chance is gone. } 10. dxc5 Qa5+ 11. Qd2 Qxc5 { Level: you won your pawn back, and the pawns are even. [%csl Bc5] }) (9... Qh5 { The queen heads for his king, but nothing is there for her yet. } 10. Be2 cxd4 11. exd4 { Level: you took on d4 one move too late, and his pieces were ready. [%csl Rh5] }) (9... Bf6 { The bishop joins the pressure, but his bishop chases your queen first. } 10. Bc4 Qd8 11. O-O { Level: his king got away to safety in time. [%csl Rd8] }) (9... Qxb3 { Look again: his a pawn and his queen both guard b3. [%cal Ra2b3,Rd1b3] } 10. Qxb3 { Lost for you: your queen is gone, and the pawn on b3 was the only prize. [%csl Rb3] }) 10. Bc4 { His bishop hits your queen, the best he has. Where does she go? } 10... Qa5+ { The queen steps out of the attack with a check, and that gains the move your rook needs to guard d4. Watch how you stay a pawn up. [%csl Gd4][%tail] } (10... Bb4+ { A check with the bishop, but his king just steps aside, and your queen is still attacked. } 11. Kf1 Qa5 12. exd4 { About level: he won his pawn back, though his king can no longer castle. [%csl Rf1] }) (10... Qf5 { The queen steps aside, but with no check, and that gives his king time to castle. } 11. O-O dxe3 12. fxe3 { About level: you are a pawn up, but his king is safe and his pieces are lively. [%csl Rf5] }) (10... Qe4 { The queen hits his bishop on f4, but his e pawn guards it, and his other bishop chases your queen away. } 11. Bd3 Qd5 12. exd4 { Level: the pawns are even again. [%csl Rd4] }) (10... Qc5 { The queen stays close, but his e pawn simply takes back on d4. } 11. exd4 Qa5+ 12. Bd2 { Level: he got the pawn back and blocked your check. [%csl Rd2] }) (10... dxe3 { Look again: your queen on d5 is attacked. } 11. Bxd5 { Lost for you: his bishop takes your queen. [%cal Rc4d5] }) (10... Qxc4 { The queen takes the bishop that attacked her, but his b pawn guards it. } 11. bxc4 { Lost for you: a queen for a bishop is a terrible trade. [%cal Rb3c4] }) (10... Qd8 { The queen goes home to safety, but without a check his e pawn has time to take back on d4. } 11. exd4 { Level: your extra pawn is gone, and his king is ready to castle. [%csl Rd4] }) 11. Nd2 Rd8 12. exd4 Rxd4 { A clean pawn up: his e pawn took yours on d4, and your rook took it straight back. [%csl Gd4] } 0-1


