{ Your queen gives check from a5, and your pawn on d4 is still alive. }
11. Qd2 { He blocks the check with his queen. That is a blunder: blocking with the knight was the right way. What now? } 11... Bb4 { Your bishop pins his queen to his king, so she cannot step away, and if she takes the bishop, your queen takes her. [%cal Gb4e1,Ga5b4][%tail] } (11... Qxd2+ { The queens come off, which is fine, but his queen was there for the taking. } 12. Kxd2 dxe3+ 13. Kxe3 { Better for you: a clean pawn up with the queens gone, and his king stands in the middle. [%csl Re3] }) (11... Nc6 { Development, but he swaps the queens and the pin is gone. } 12. Qxa5 Nxa5 13. Bd3 { Better for you: a pawn up, but his queen got away. [%csl Ga5] }) (11... dxe3 { Look again: your queen on a5 is attacked by his queen. } 12. Qxa5 { Lost for you: his queen simply takes yours on a5. [%cal Rd2a5] }) 12. exd4 Bxd2+ 13. Bxd2 Qd8 { His queen is gone, and you only gave a bishop for her. Your own queen is safe on d8. [%csl Gd8] } 0-1


