[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnb2rk1/pp2bppp/4p3/3q4/3p1B2/1P2PN2/P4PPP/R2QKB1R_w_KQ_-_0_10"]

{ Your c pawn has just taken on d4, and your queen still stands on d5. }
10. Qxd4 { His queen takes back on d4. That is a blunder: hitting your queen with the bishop to c4 was his move, and taking back with the knight fails too. What now? } 10... Qa5+ { Check! Your queen steps out of reach of his, and his own queen is left out in the middle, where your pieces can hit her. [%cal Ga5e1] } (10... Qxd4 { The natural trade, but his queen was the one in trouble, and trading her off sets him free. } 11. Nxd4 Bf6 12. Bxb8 Rxb8 { About level: the queens are gone, and so is your chance. [%csl Rd4] }) (10... Qh5 { The queen steps aside, and his queen gets out of trouble too. } 11. Qe5 Bb4+ 12. Nd2 { Better for you: his king is stuck in the middle, but his queen got away. [%csl Ge5] }) (10... Qf5 { The queen steps aside to f5, but with no check, so his bishop gets a free move to hit her from d3. } 11. Bd3 Qa5+ 12. Ke2 { About level: the check comes one move too late, and his queen is safe. [%csl Rd4] }) (10... Bb4+ { Look again: his queen on d4 takes your bishop on b4 along the row. } 11. Qxb4 { Lost for you: his queen took your bishop, and she is safe on b4. [%csl Rb4] }) (10... Rd8 { The rook joins the d column, but his queen takes yours before anything happens. } 11. Qxd5 exd5 { Level: the queens are off, and his blunder cost him nothing. [%csl Gd5] }) (10... Nc6 { The knight hits his queen, but she just trades herself for yours on d5. } 11. Qxd5 exd5 { Level: the queens came off, and the chance to win his queen went with them. [%csl Gc6] }) 11. Nd2 { His knight blocks the check. What now? } 11... Bf6 { Your bishop hits his queen, and his rook in the corner stands right behind her. [%cal Gf6a1][%tail] } (11... Rd8 { Just as good: the rook hits his queen, and if she leaves the d column, your rook takes his pinned knight on d2. } 12. Qxd8+ Qxd8 { Much better for you: you gave a rook for his queen. [%csl Gd8] }) (11... Nc6 { The knight hits his queen too, but she gets away to a4 and hits yours. } 12. Qa4 Qc3 { Much better for you: your queen hits his rook in the corner, and his knight on d2 is still pinned. [%cal Gc3a1] }) (11... Bb4 { The bishop piles up on his pinned knight, but his rook comes over to guard it. } 12. Rd1 { Much better for you, but his rook now guards the knight, and his queen is still on the board. [%csl Rd1] }) (11... e5 { The pawn hits his queen and bishop, but his queen takes it and offers a trade. } 12. Qxe5 Qxe5 13. Bxe5 { Level: the queens are off, and his bishop took your e pawn. [%csl Re5] }) 12. Qxf6 gxf6 { A queen up for a bishop: he gave his queen rather than let your bishop take the rook behind her. [%csl Gf6] } 0-1
